{
  "dom_id": "step-q338488",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Let $F(x)=2x$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $3$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,3)$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f(x+6)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n\nII. $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n\nIII. $f(x+2)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$",
  "worked_solution": "Since $f$ is the $3$-periodic extension of $F,$ we have \n\n- $f(x+3)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,3).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+6)=f((x+3)+3)=f(x+3)=f(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, take $x=4.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4)=f(1+3)=f(1)=F(1)=2(1)=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(4)=2(4)=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)\\ne F(x)$ for $x=4.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, take $x=2.$ Then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2+2)=f(4)=f(1+3)=f(1)=F(1)=2(1)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2)=F(2)=2(2)=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x+2)\\ne f(2)$ for $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\".",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
